        <title type="html"><![CDATA[FF155 Relnote: WebGPU dual-source-blending]]></title>
        <id>https://github.com/mdn/content/pull/45207</id>
        <link href="https://github.com/mdn/content/pull/45207"/>
        <updated>2026-08-18T01:31:33.000Z</updated>
        <content type="html"><![CDATA[<img src="https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/5368500?v=4" width="64" height="64" alt=""/><br/><p dir="auto">FF155 supports the <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/GPUSupportedFeatures#available_features" rel="nofollow">dual-source blending</a> feature in <a href="https://bugzilla.mozilla.org/show_bug.cgi?id=1924328" rel="nofollow">https://bugzilla.mozilla.org/show_bug.cgi?id=1924328</a></p>
<p dir="auto">This adds the feature for Firefox desktop (FF android doesn't support the parent feature).</p>
<p dir="auto">Note that I have explained what it allows you to specifically use, rather than what it allows you to do - because that's what the existing docs do, and because I don't know enough about Web GPU to be sure of what AIs tell me.<br>
According to Gemini this allows you to blend two sources into a destination texture: your source and a per-channel mask. This allows control over the mixing of your source into particular colour channels rather than equally to all channels. Apprarently can be useful for some fonts etc.</p>
<p dir="auto">Related docs work can be tracked in <a class="issue-link js-issue-link" data-error-text="Failed to load title" data-id="5170075051" data-permission-text="Title is private" data-url="https://github.com/mdn/content/issues/45176" data-hovercard-type="issue" data-hovercard-url="/mdn/content/issues/45176/hovercard" href="https://github.com/mdn/content/issues/45176">#45176</a></p>]]></content>
